WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.856
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:08.026 --> 00:00:10.610
LAURA KIESEL: I have what I guess is called invisible illness,

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:12.050
so I don't look sick.

00:00:12.050 --> 00:00:16.650
I've had this problem, even when I had my endometriosis was really bad.

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:17.810
And I was getting--

00:00:17.810 --> 00:00:20.120
I was having a lot of gastrointestinal issues.

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:21.890
I was losing weight.

00:00:21.890 --> 00:00:24.800
I remember going to my--

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:28.140
this was when I was in college-- going to the doctors there,

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:30.570
and them just not believing I was sick.

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:32.479
They accused me of being bulimic.

00:00:32.479 --> 00:00:33.920
They were like, you probably just don't want to eat.

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:35.820
And I was like, no, I'm sick to my stomach.

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:36.903
I don't know what's wrong.

00:00:36.903 --> 00:00:37.945
I'm in pain all the time.

00:00:37.945 --> 00:00:39.819
But they were just like, you don't look sick.

00:00:39.819 --> 00:00:41.210
Look at how healthy your hair is.

00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:44.600
No one who is in pain could have hair as nice as you.

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:47.060
I've been told that by doctors.

00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:51.110
And I've had similar things not as egregious as that as I've gotten older,

00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:53.000
words like, well, you can walk.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:56.000
And you're not in a wheelchair.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:59.190
And you have nice teeth, or you have nice hair.

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:01.340
Or you'e too young to have back problems.

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:05.269
I've had one doctor say, thin people don't have the back problems

00:01:05.269 --> 00:01:06.440
you seem to be having.

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:08.630
And it's like, well, I have those back problems.

00:01:08.630 --> 00:01:11.360
So you see my MRI.

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:12.620
You see I have disc issues.

00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:18.530
So there's a lot of-- there's new advocacy around this idea of just

00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:21.920
because you can't plainly see that a person is in pain

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:26.240
or is disabled that doesn't mean they don't have illness or disability.

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:29.270
But it's still very hard, and I sometimes feel like even with doctors

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:31.730
I have to really argue with them.

00:01:31.730 --> 00:01:33.800
Like, I'm in a lot of pain.

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:38.180
And even though I'm standing here, and I'm not crying,

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:38.680
and I'm not in a wheelchair,

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:42.050
I'm in pain.

00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:45.740
And then there's the 1 through 10 thing--

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:47.600
tell me how much pain you're in.

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:51.170
And it's really hard if you live with chronic pain as your daily reality,

00:01:51.170 --> 00:01:53.420
because it&amp;#39;s harder to gauge.

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:56.090
Because it's like, well, this is my daily reality.

00:01:56.090 --> 00:01:58.100
You kind of adapt a little to the pain.

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:00.110
And it's like, well, if this pain was brand new,

00:02:00.110 --> 00:02:01.700
I'd be in the emergency room.

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:03.830
But I live like this every day.

00:02:03.830 --> 00:02:08.389
So I guess it's for me now a 5, but when I first started getting it,

00:02:08.389 --> 00:02:10.130
it was an 8 or a 9.

00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:11.180
But it's still bad.

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:13.820
It's just I've lived with it every day.

00:02:13.820 --> 00:02:15.920
And I know now I'm not dying from it.

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:20.450
But so those 1 to 10 things can be really deceiving,

00:02:20.450 --> 00:02:23.750
because you don't even know what to tell doctors when it's something

00:02:23.750 --> 00:02:26.440
you live with every single day.

